      "writtenOn": "2022-03-11T04:55:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Not directly related to this CL.\nI assume ESPI_VWIRE_SIGNAL_PLTRST of this event notifies there is chipset reset(the new name is AP_POWER_RESET). Also zephyr driver(like NPCX) has reset event. Isn\u0027t the reset coming from ap_pwrseq?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 68,
        "startChar": 6,
        "endLine": 68,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "6476411d84d0a8ebf316d34f34abebaa0591ac1f",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "44ad8211_12d793e6",
        "filename": "zephyr/subsys/ap_pwrseq/signal_vw.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 68,
      "author": {
        "id": 1281040
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-03-11T05:26:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So chipset_reset is a function in the legacy API (ec/include/chipset.h) that gets called to actually reset the AP.\nSo this is shimmed into ap_power_reset in chromium:3515426\n\nThis is different from the legacy HOOK_CHIPSET_RESET (or the new AP_POWER_RESET), which is a notification that the CPU has been reset.\nAnd yes, I see that shim/src/espi.c there is espi_chipset_reset, and in chromium:3518465 this get converted to use the ap_power_event.\nIn the legacy EC code, there only place that calls hook_notify on HOOK_CHIPSET_RESET is the espi devices, so this seems OK.\nI can\u0027t see anywhere in the legacy power sequence code that sends HOOK_CHIPSET_RESET as a notification.",
      "parentUuid": "f0933a9d_fbaa68b6",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 68,
        "startChar": 6,
        "endLine": 68,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "6476411d84d0a8ebf316d34f34abebaa0591ac1f",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d04da58e_e3c9e8d1",
        "filename": "zephyr/subsys/ap_pwrseq/signal_vw.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 68,
      "author": {
        "id": 1281040
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-03-11T05:37:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "And just to expand, none of the existing EC code references ESPI_VWIRE_SIGNAL_PLTRST at all. The ESPI devices use the notification that the ESPI bus has been reset to generate the reset event.\nI don\u0027t know enough about this system to know if that is correct or not.",
      "parentUuid": "44ad8211_12d793e6",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 68,
        "startChar": 6,
        "endLine": 68,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "6476411d84d0a8ebf316d34f34abebaa0591ac1f",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3080bd05_3be3e485",
        "filename": "zephyr/subsys/ap_pwrseq/signal_vw.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 68,
      "author": {
        "id": 1281040
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-03-11T05:39:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "And to expand even further :-) this particular handler (signal_vw) is only using the ESPI bus reset as an indicator that the virtual wire signals will not be valid until new values are received - that\u0027s the only use of the ESPI bus reset callback in this file.",
      "parentUuid": "d04da58e_e3c9e8d1",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 68,
        "startChar": 6,
        "endLine": 68,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "6476411d84d0a8ebf316d34f34abebaa0591ac1f",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4d772a2d_14145758",
        "filename": "zephyr/subsys/ap_pwrseq/signal_vw.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 68,
      "author": {
        "id": 1116125
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-03-11T05:50:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "ESPI_BUS_RESET is different, not the reset I meant here.\nI\u0027m wondering how we get chipset reset notification, like EC \"apreset\", or AP \"reboot\". ESPI_BUS_EVENT_VWIRE_RECEIVED signals VW channels like ESPI_VWIRE_SIGNAL_SLP_S3/S4/S5 has event, and ESPI_VWIRE_SIGNAL_PLTRST is one of the channel too. I know for sure ESPI_VWIRE_SIGNAL_PLTRST is signaled when I do \"apreset\" or AP \"reboot\". My understanding is ap_pwrseq will notify modules who registers SOC reset/or platform reset(not sure which is the right name) by ESPI_VWIRE_SIGNAL_PLTRST signaling.\nIn Shim zephyr code, chipset reset hook never happen even \"aprest\" or AP \"reboot\". maybe because it doesn\u0027t break any functionality so not noticed.\nMy point is if ap_pwrseq is the right place to send SOC reset notification, then we need to consider add the handling here.\nIf Keith can confirm this will be good.",
      "parentUuid": "d04da58e_e3c9e8d1",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 68,
        "startChar": 6,
        "endLine": 68,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "6476411d84d0a8ebf316d34f34abebaa0591ac1f",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "78478529_501ec6d0",
        "filename": "zephyr/subsys/ap_pwrseq/signal_vw.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 68,
      "author": {
        "id": 1281040
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-03-11T06:03:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I understood what you meant (in terms of the ESPI bus reset vs the CPU reset).\nThe ESPI driver (both the legacy and the shim drivers) are the ones that are currently using the ESPI_BUS_RESET to presumably detect the CPU has been reset, and to then use that to notify about the CPU reset.\n\nSo you ask a good question, in that it is may be better for the ESPI drivers to use ESPI_VWIRE_SIGNAL_PLTRST rather than the ESPI bus reset to detect when the CPU has been reset.\nI do note that this action is guarded by CONFIG_PLATFORM_EC_CHIPSET_RESET_HOOK, but that by default it is enabled.\nI can\u0027t comment on what happens when \u0027apreset\u0027 or CPU reboot happens in terms of the ESPI driver. It may be worth putting in some debug to discover exactly when the ESPI bus reset occurs, and whether it is a reliable way to detect CPU reset.",
      "parentUuid": "4d772a2d_14145758",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 68,
        "startChar": 6,
        "endLine": 68,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "6476411d84d0a8ebf316d34f34abebaa0591ac1f",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    }
